**Current support and accepted design.** Template directives work today. Source directives such as $config are planned. See @../progress (Progress) for current support.

A directive modifies an existing construct. Template-head directives modify their template and source directives modify the following eligible construct.

### The two locations

Place a template directive in the template head to modify that template:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    message #= [$md: Hello from Moth]
]

Place a source directive immediately before the declaration or statement it modifies. The following $config example is accepted design and is not yet implemented:

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    api_url #String = "https://example.com"
]

Source directives must remain attached to their target. Comment-only lines may intervene, but a blank line breaks attachment. A directive at the start of a file still modifies the following construct.

### Directive operands

A directive may have one optional compile-time operand in the form $name, $name value or $name key = value. It cannot have a type annotation or a function-call-style argument list. Template heads retain their own slot names and separators.

Module entry selection uses the fixed @entry.moth filename, not a directive. Project and builder settings stay as named constants inside config.moth. The current typed input spelling is #Config of T until $config is implemented.

See @../directives (Directives), @../project-structure/#project-config (Project config) and @../project-structure/#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).
